{"id": 17, "ts": 1783923239.1046336, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_1", "message": "I appreciate the focus on crew cooperation and communication, which are indeed decisive for safety. While Candidate\u202fC brings impressive technical and cognitive strengths, we still lack concrete evidence that this translates into effective cockpit collaboration, especially given the noted reluctance to accept feedback. Candidate\u202fB, on the other hand, is described as very conscientious, handles stress well, and assesses weather accurately\u2014key for reliability and stress resilience. However, the notes also flag B as sometimes grumpy, uncooperative, and pretentious, with a tendency to adopt the wrong tone when communicating. Without specific examples, it is difficult to gauge whether these attitude issues would undermine crew cooperation in practice.\nGiven the current evidence, I am less convinced that Candidate\u202fC\u2019s technical edge outweighs the uncertainty around their teamwork, and I am also not fully persuaded that Candidate\u202fB\u2019s interpersonal concerns are manageable. Therefore, I will provisionally recommend **Candidate\u202fA**. A is noted as 100\u202f% reliable and creates a positive atmosphere with his crew, directly addressing the crew\u2011cooperation criterion. Although A has difficulty communicating ideas, is seen as egocentric, and is not eager to pursue further education, these concerns are less safety\u2011critical than potential uncooperative behavior that could impair decision\u2011making under pressure. A\u2019s proven reliability and positive crew environment give a solid foundation for the long\u2011distance role, provided we can support his communication development."}
